1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,444
What is a merge?

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,297
Merging brings the work of one branch into another.

3
00:00:10,297 --> 00:00:17,279
We see the two shapes a merge can produce, and that merging into the current branch is always the direction.

4
00:00:19,033 --> 00:00:23,433
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

5
00:00:23,967 --> 00:00:26,469
On main, merge feature-tea:

6
00:00:26,469 --> 00:00:29,807
ort is Git's default merge strategy.

7
00:00:29,807 --> 00:00:30,826
The result:

8
00:00:31,367 --> 00:00:34,438
ort is Git's default merge strategy.

9
00:00:34,438 --> 00:00:35,376
The result:

10
00:00:35,376 --> 00:00:37,594
main now has both changes:

11
00:00:37,594 --> 00:00:42,968
green tea (from feature-tea) and the opening hours (from main).

12
00:00:42,968 --> 00:00:45,101
feature-tea did not move.

13
00:00:45,667 --> 00:00:47,845
main now has both changes:

14
00:00:47,845 --> 00:00:53,122
green tea (from feature-tea) and the opening hours (from main).

15
00:00:53,122 --> 00:00:55,217
feature-tea did not move.

16
00:00:55,217 --> 00:00:57,981
The merge commit has two parents:

17
00:00:59,733 --> 00:01:01,191
Let me try something.

18
00:01:01,191 --> 00:01:03,204
Merge in the wrong direction:

19
00:01:03,204 --> 00:01:08,063
you meant to bring main into your feature branch, but you are on main.

20
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:10,026
Already up to date.:

21
00:01:10,026 --> 00:01:15,230
main already contains everything of feature-tea (the merge you just did).

22
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:16,799
The direction matters:

23
00:01:16,799 --> 00:01:19,579
git merge X changes the current branch.

24
00:01:19,579 --> 00:01:24,854
To update feature-tea with main's latest work, you must be on feature-tea.

25
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:29,946
feature-tea now has main's work too.

26
00:01:29,946 --> 00:01:32,084
(It was a fast-forward:

27
00:01:32,084 --> 00:01:33,200
lesson 24.).

28
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:35,647
Got it.

29
00:01:35,647 --> 00:01:38,398
What do I need to remember?

30
00:01:38,933 --> 00:01:45,523
git merge X brings X into the current branch; the current branch moves, X does not.

31
00:01:46,067 --> 00:01:50,181
A merge commit has two parents and combines both histories.

32
00:01:50,733 --> 00:01:54,077
Check git branch --show-current before merging:

33
00:01:54,077 --> 00:01:55,358
direction matters.

34
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:00,635
I keep hearing about fast-forward merge.

35
00:02:00,635 --> 00:02:02,314
What is it, really?

36
00:02:02,867 --> 00:02:10,691
When the current branch has not moved since the other branch started, a merge does not need a merge commit:

37
00:02:10,691 --> 00:02:13,251
Git simply moves the label forward.

38
00:02:13,251 --> 00:02:14,933
That is a fast-forward.

39
00:02:14,933 --> 00:02:20,271
We also see how to refuse it (--no-ff) and how to require it (--ff-only).

40
00:02:22,033 --> 00:02:26,433
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

41
00:02:26,967 --> 00:02:27,963
Fast-forward:

42
00:02:27,963 --> 00:02:32,791
no merge commit; main and feature-tea point to the same commit.

43
00:02:32,791 --> 00:02:35,396
The history stays a straight line.

44
00:02:35,933 --> 00:02:36,930
Fast-forward:

45
00:02:36,930 --> 00:02:41,757
no merge commit; main and feature-tea point to the same commit.

46
00:02:41,757 --> 00:02:44,363
The history stays a straight line.

47
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:47,793
Let me try something.

48
00:02:47,793 --> 00:02:51,179
--ff-only when the branches have diverged:

49
00:02:51,733 --> 00:02:52,192
fatal:

50
00:02:52,192 --> 00:02:55,246
Not possible to fast-forward, aborting.:

51
00:02:55,246 --> 00:03:02,806
main has a commit (Add chai) that feature-mocha does not, so main cannot simply move forward to it.

52
00:03:02,806 --> 00:03:04,868
The branches have diverged:

53
00:03:06,633 --> 00:03:08,090
Two valid choices:

54
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:11,893
allow a merge commit (git merge feature-mocha),

55
00:03:11,893 --> 00:03:19,743
or first replay the branch on top of main so a fast-forward becomes possible (rebase, lesson 61).

56
00:03:19,743 --> 00:03:21,038
With the rebase:

57
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:23,513
Got it.

58
00:03:23,513 --> 00:03:26,264
What do I need to remember?

59
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:27,873
Fast-forward:

60
00:03:27,873 --> 00:03:33,323
the current branch had not moved, so its label just moves forward.

61
00:03:33,323 --> 00:03:34,644
No merge commit.

62
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:41,159
--no-ff forces a merge commit; --ff-only refuses anything but a fast-forward.

63
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:44,709
Diverged branches cannot fast-forward:

64
00:03:44,709 --> 00:03:47,639
merge with a commit, or rebase first.

65
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:51,819
How does three-way merge work?

66
00:03:52,367 --> 00:03:55,659
How Git merges two branches that both changed:

67
00:03:55,659 --> 00:04:01,671
it compares three snapshots, the two tips and their common ancestor, the merge base.

68
00:04:03,433 --> 00:04:07,833
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

69
00:04:08,367 --> 00:04:09,754
The three snapshots:

70
00:04:09,754 --> 00:04:16,136
Each side changed a different file, so Git can take each change from its side automatically:

71
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:22,825
Each side changed a different file, so Git can take each change from its side automatically:

72
00:04:24,567 --> 00:04:26,232
Let me try something.

73
00:04:26,232 --> 00:04:30,196
Merge a branch that is not related to main at all:

74
00:04:30,196 --> 00:04:31,306
no merge base.

75
00:04:31,867 --> 00:04:34,329
refusing to merge unrelated histories:

76
00:04:34,329 --> 00:04:40,938
unrelated has no commit in common with main (it was started with --orphan), so there is no merge base.

77
00:04:40,938 --> 00:04:49,555
In real life this appears when you connect a local project to a GitHub repository that was created with a README (two first commits),

78
00:04:49,555 --> 00:04:52,406
or when two different projects are combined.

79
00:04:54,167 --> 00:04:58,601
If combining them is really intended, allow it explicitly:

80
00:05:00,367 --> 00:05:01,080
Got it.

81
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:03,831
What do I need to remember?

82
00:05:04,367 --> 00:05:08,376
A three-way merge compares the two tips with their merge base.

83
00:05:08,933 --> 00:05:15,043
Changes on one side, or on different lines of the same file, merge automatically.

84
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:22,200
No merge base = unrelated histories; --allow-unrelated-histories only when you really mean it.

85
00:05:23,933 --> 00:05:26,478
Why would I need merge conflicts?

86
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:34,238
What a merge conflict is, why it happens, and how to read the conflict markers Git writes into the file.

87
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,399
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

88
00:05:40,933 --> 00:05:42,720
The merge stopped.

89
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:44,408
Look at the file:

90
00:05:44,967 --> 00:05:46,382
The merge stopped.

91
00:05:46,382 --> 00:05:47,719
Look at the file:

92
00:05:47,719 --> 00:05:55,661
Everything outside the markers merged fine (espresso, cappuccino); only the contested line is marked.

93
00:05:56,200 --> 00:06:04,557
Everything outside the markers merged fine (espresso, cappuccino); only the contested line is marked.

94
00:06:04,557 --> 00:06:13,494
The base version can be shown too, which makes the decision easier ("what did it say before both changes?"):

95
00:06:13,494 --> 00:06:17,134
||||||| introduces the merge base's version:

96
00:06:17,134 --> 00:06:18,045
latte 3.20.

97
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:21,515
Let me try something.

98
00:06:21,515 --> 00:06:24,620
Ignore the conflict and try to commit:

99
00:06:25,167 --> 00:06:29,157
Committing is not possible because you have unmerged files.:

100
00:06:29,157 --> 00:06:33,280
Git will not record a merge while a file is still in conflict.

101
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:35,541
git status explains where you are:

102
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:39,840
Resolution is lesson 27.

103
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:44,920
For now, step back out of the merge (lesson 28):

104
00:06:46,667 --> 00:06:47,380
Got it.

105
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:50,131
What do I need to remember?

106
00:06:50,667 --> 00:06:57,001
A conflict = the same lines changed differently on both sides; Git stops and asks you.

107
00:06:57,567 --> 00:07:08,521
<<<<<<< HEAD (yours) / ======= / >>>>>>> branch (theirs); ||||||| shows the base.

108
00:07:09,067 --> 00:07:15,476
You cannot commit until every conflict is resolved; git status lists them as "both modified".

109
00:07:17,233 --> 00:07:20,683
What should I know about resolving merge conflicts?

110
00:07:21,233 --> 00:07:22,678
The resolution loop:

111
00:07:22,678 --> 00:07:27,735
decide the right content, edit the file, git add it, commit the merge.

112
00:07:27,735 --> 00:07:32,648
And the shortcuts for "take mine" or "take theirs" for a whole file.

113
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:38,800
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

114
00:07:39,333 --> 00:07:40,741
The conflict:

115
00:07:40,741 --> 00:07:44,855
main says 3.30, feature-tea says 3.50.

116
00:07:44,855 --> 00:07:52,108
After asking the team, the decision is 3.40, which is neither side.

117
00:07:52,667 --> 00:07:54,441
Mark it resolved and commit:

118
00:07:54,441 --> 00:07:59,637
--no-edit keeps Git's prepared message ("Merge branch 'feature-tea'"); without it,

119
00:07:59,637 --> 00:08:05,086
your editor opens with that message, where you can add a line explaining the decision.

120
00:08:06,833 --> 00:08:08,361
Let me try something.

121
00:08:08,361 --> 00:08:09,742
Resolve carelessly:

122
00:08:09,742 --> 00:08:14,033
"resolve" by staging the file with the markers still in it.

123
00:08:14,567 --> 00:08:15,749
Git accepted it:

124
00:08:15,749 --> 00:08:19,148
git add means "resolved" whatever the content.

125
00:08:19,148 --> 00:08:24,543
The markers are now committed, and anything that reads prices.txt breaks.

126
00:08:24,543 --> 00:08:27,646
Find committed markers before they spread:

127
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:31,442
The merge is not pushed:

128
00:08:31,442 --> 00:08:32,889
redo it properly.

129
00:08:32,889 --> 00:08:37,740
Undo the bad merge commit, merge again, resolve for real:

130
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:40,213
Got it.

131
00:08:40,213 --> 00:08:42,965
What do I need to remember?

132
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:50,045
Resolve = write the correct content, remove every marker, git add, git commit.

133
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:53,970
--ours/--theirs take one side's whole file.

134
00:08:54,533 --> 00:08:55,973
git add trusts you:

135
00:08:55,973 --> 00:09:01,503
check for leftover markers (git diff --check) and test before committing.

136
00:09:03,267 --> 00:09:05,816
Can you show me aborting a merge?

137
00:09:06,367 --> 00:09:11,436
git merge --abort returns you to exactly where you were before a merge started.

138
00:09:11,436 --> 00:09:16,826
Useful when a conflict is bigger than expected, or when you merged the wrong branch.

139
00:09:18,567 --> 00:09:22,966
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

140
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:33,620
While a merge is in progress, Git keeps MERGE_HEAD (the commit being merged) and your prompt or git status says "You have unmerged paths".

141
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:34,060
Abort:

142
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:44,740
While a merge is in progress, Git keeps MERGE_HEAD (the commit being merged) and your prompt or git status says "You have unmerged paths".

143
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:45,181
Abort:

144
00:09:45,181 --> 00:09:48,194
Back to the exact state before the merge:

145
00:09:48,194 --> 00:09:51,795
same commit, clean working directory, no markers.

146
00:09:53,533 --> 00:09:55,056
Let me try something.

147
00:09:55,056 --> 00:09:58,753
Start a merge with uncommitted work in your folder.

148
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:04,335
Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by merge:

149
00:10:04,335 --> 00:10:08,180
Git refuses to start, protecting your uncommitted edit.

150
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:11,397
Nothing was merged; there is nothing to abort.

151
00:10:11,397 --> 00:10:12,026
The rule:

152
00:10:12,026 --> 00:10:18,390
start merges from a clean working directory, so that --abort can always restore everything.

153
00:10:20,133 --> 00:10:28,518
Put the draft aside (lesson 35, git stash), merge, abort if needed, bring the draft back:

154
00:10:30,267 --> 00:10:30,980
Got it.

155
00:10:30,980 --> 00:10:33,731
What do I need to remember?

156
00:10:34,267 --> 00:10:38,166
git merge --abort restores the state before the merge started.

157
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:43,810
Start merges with a clean working directory (commit or stash first).

158
00:10:44,367 --> 00:10:47,851
MERGE_HEAD exists while a merge is in progress.
